course: adult-life
lesson: 39
title: Money, work and the big choices
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Growing up brings big choices: how to look after money, what work to do, which exams and courses to take, when to learn to drive and whom to vote for.
explain: None of them is made in one day; people save bit by bit, start with small jobs, revise for weeks and change their minds as they learn what they like.
explain: Vivid example: in Toronto, big sister Maya opens her first pay slip from the cinema, shouts "I'm RICH!", then goes quiet: "Hang on, where's the rest? What's all this tax?" Dad laughs and explains where the missing money went.
explain: The money taken off pays for schools, hospitals and roads, and some is saved for when she is old.
explain: Knowing how money, work and choices really work now makes the big steps much less scary later.
fact: A bank account keeps your money safe; a savings account pays you a little extra, called interest, for leaving money in it.
fact: Small spends like coffees and snacks add up fast; managing money means knowing what comes in and what goes out.
fact: Saving means putting money aside now for something bigger later, or for emergencies like a broken boiler.
fact: A payslip shows what you earned and what was taken off, such as tax and pension, before the rest reached your bank.
fact: Tax pays for schools, hospitals and roads; a pension is money saved for when you are old.
fact: A first job is often small and part-time, like a Saturday job at sixteen; it teaches being on time, taking instructions and earning your own money.
fact: A CV is a short, honest document about your skills, results and work, which employers read to decide whom to meet.
fact: An interview is a meeting where an employer asks questions to see if you suit a job, and you see if the job suits you; most people don't get every job they try for.
fact: Exams like GCSEs and A levels open doors later, but people who do badly can retake them or find other routes; steady work beats panicking the night before.
fact: Choosing a job means thinking about what you enjoy, what you're good at and what pays enough; many adults change jobs or careers more than once.
fact: University means studying a subject for a few years and often leaves a big loan; an apprenticeship means learning a job at work while being paid.
fact: In the UK you can start learning to drive at seventeen and must pass a theory test and a practical test; L plates show a learner.
fact: Voting is choosing who runs the country or the local council; in the UK most people can vote from eighteen, and grown-ups are allowed to disagree about whom to vote for.
exercise: Gran helps you open a savings account. What extra does it pay you for leaving money there? | Interest | Tax | Rent | A pension
exercise: Mum stares at her banking app: "Where has all the money gone?" What usually explains it? | Lots of small spends that added up | The bank stole it overnight | Money slowly disappears by itself | The app was showing the wrong year
exercise: Dad says, "We're saving up for the holiday, so no takeaway this week." What does saving mean? | Putting money aside now for something later | Spending it all now before prices rise again next year | Borrowing money from the bank | Hiding money under the bed forever
exercise: Why do grown-ups also save for emergencies? | So a surprise bill, like a broken boiler, isn't a disaster | Because the banks make every family save a bit every week | So they can buy a new car every year | Because money is worth more if it's old
exercise: Your big sister shows her first payslip. What does it show? | What she earned and what was taken off | The address of her boss's house | How many hours she slept that month and when she woke up | A list of things she must buy
exercise: What is a pension? | Money saved for when you are old | A tax that pays for roads | A bonus for working on Saturdays | A loan for going to university
exercise: What does tax pay for? | Schools, hospitals and roads | The boss's holiday and car | Your own pocket money | Only the price of the payslip paper
exercise: Your cousin starts a Saturday job at the café at sixteen. What does a first job like this teach? | Being on time, taking instructions and earning money | How to run the whole café alone, from the till to the kitchen | Nothing useful for later jobs | How to avoid ever working again
exercise: Your big brother types up his CV. What is a CV? | A short honest document about your skills and work | A list of all the jobs you would hate to do and why you hate them | A secret form only teachers fill in | A letter saying you want a pay rise
exercise: Mum irons a shirt: "I've got an interview tomorrow." What is an interview? | A meeting where an employer asks questions about a job | A test of how well you can iron | A chat to tell your boss you're leaving and why you are going | A meeting to sign a flat rental contract
exercise: Big sister revises late with highlighters everywhere. Which is true about exams? | They matter, but you can retake them or find other routes | If you fail one, your life is ruined | They are just for fun and don't count | Only the night before matters, so you can leave all the revising till then
exercise: Uncle sighs, "I wish I'd chosen a job I actually enjoy." Which is true? | Many adults change jobs or careers more than once | Once you pick a job, you can never change | Enjoying your job is impossible for adults | Only children are allowed to change their minds
exercise: Your cousin is choosing between university and an apprenticeship. What is an apprenticeship? | Learning a job at work while being paid | Studying at a college for years with a big loan | A holiday before starting work | A test you take to leave school
exercise: Big sister grips the wheel with L plates on the car. What do the L plates show? | She is a learner driver | She has just passed her test | The car belongs to a lawyer | The car is too large for the road
exercise: In the UK, what must a learner pass to get a driving licence? | A theory test and a practical test | Only a short eye test | A maths exam and a spelling test at the post office | Nothing, you just turn seventeen
exercise: Mum comes back from the polling station with a sticker. What has she done? | She has voted | She has posted a parcel | She has paid a fine | She has seen a doctor
test: Dad, on the phone to the bank: "So if I leave it in for a year, how much interest do I get?" What is he asking? | How much extra the bank will pay him for saving | How much tax he owes the government at the end of each year | How much he must pay to close the account | How much the bank charges for a card
test: Mum, scrolling: "£4 coffee, £3 coffee, £5 sandwich... bloody hell, that's £60 this week!" What is she learning? | Small spends add up fast | Coffee prices went up by £60 | The bank made a mistake | Sandwiches are free on Fridays
test: Your little brother says, "Maya's job pays £10 an hour, so all the money goes straight in her pocket." Put him right. | No, tax and pension are taken off first | Yes, every penny goes straight to her | No, her boss keeps half for himself | Yes, but only after she turns twenty
test: Overheard: "Boiler's packed in, thank God we had a bit put by." What saved them? | Money they had saved for emergencies | A loan from the neighbours next door | The boiler company paying for it all as a favour | A lucky lottery ticket
test: Cousin, sixteen: "Café job's dead boring but I got paid, and I'm never late now." What has the job taught her? | Being on time and earning her own money | That all jobs are always boring | That being late is fine at the café as long as you smile | How to cook the café's whole menu
test: Big brother: "I'll just say I've got a degree on my CV, nobody checks, innit." What's wrong with that? | A CV must be honest | A CV must be handwritten | A CV must be in French | Nothing, everyone does that
test: Mum, after a phone call: "Didn't get it. Ah well, onto the next one." What is true? | Most people don't get every job they try for | She will never get a job now | Interviews are just for practice | The employer made a terrible mistake and will ring her back to say sorry
test: Big sister, sobbing: "I failed maths, my life's over!" What would a good parent say? | You can retake it or find another route | Yes, your life is over now | Exams don't matter at all, so just forget about it | You should never study maths again
test: Cousin: "Uni means a massive loan, but apprentices get paid from day one." Is that right? | Yes, that's true | No, apprentices pay to work | No, university is always free | Yes, but apprentices never get jobs
test: Grandad and Dad argue about whom to vote for at the election. What does this show? | Grown-ups are allowed to disagree about voting | Only one of them is allowed to vote | They will both be fined by the council for arguing | Voting is a family decision by law
test: Big sister, sixteen: "I'm getting driving lessons for my birthday next week!" In the UK, what's the problem? | You can only start learning at seventeen | You need to be twenty-one to learn | Driving lessons are only for boys | There is no problem at all with that
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises the deductions itemised upon a payslip. | Sums such as tax and pension withheld prior to payment | Charges levied by the bank for holding the account | Penalties imposed for late arrival at work | Contributions to the employer's personal savings
exam: Identify the principal distinction between a university course and an apprenticeship as established in this lesson. | An apprentice learns a trade at work while being paid | A university student is paid a wage from the outset | An apprenticeship confers no prospect of a career | A university course involves no financial cost
exam: It is given that an adult is unsuccessful in an application for employment. Determine the conclusion consistent with the established facts. | This is commonplace, and further applications may succeed | The adult is thereby excluded from all future employment | The interviewer is obliged to reconsider the decision | Interviews are of no consequence in obtaining work
exam: Ascertain the purpose served by the taxation deducted from an employee's earnings. | The funding of public services such as schools and hospitals | The enlargement of the employer's personal income and of the company's profits | The payment of the employee's own pension in full | The purchase of the employee's travel to work
